the biden administration is nearing a  decision on student loan forgiveness an  issue that could affect millions of  americans and influence the coming  midterm elections  no matter what he ends up deciding biden  will be taking a major political risk  whether he decides to cancel large  amounts of student debt ten thousand  dollars worth of student debt or no  student debt at all it's going to anger  somebody and it's unclear whether that  somebody will be voting in november  biden has already ruled out for giving  fifty thousand dollars per student  borrower but has shown support for  legislation eliminating up to ten  thousand dollars in debt  still some legal experts and economists  say the administration needs a plan that  will stand up to legal challenges and  avoid contributing to record high  inflation rates as the debate over mass  student loan forgiveness continues what  are some of the key challenges  complicating biden's final decision  so rather than taking a stroke of the  pen and saying  all student debt is canceled biden has  instead used existing powers that the  federal government already has for  disabled borrowers for students who have  been defrauded for people who work in  public service as biden considers  whether to cancel debt on a mass scale  he has to juggle legal political and  economic fallout that might result from  that kind of decision one concern tied  to mass student loan forgiveness is  inflation  some economists warn debt cancellation  could intensify price pressures at a  time when inflation is at its highest  level in 40 years with an 8.6 annual  rate as of may  there are worries that canceling student  debt on a mass scale could exacerbate  problems with inflation because it would  give borrowers more spending power which  they would then use to purchase goods  and drive up inflation further in a  supply-constrained economy in order to  prevent that the byte administration is  looking at these lower levels of debt  forgiveness which would be less  inflationary depending on what  forgiveness policy biden lands on the  price tag could range anywhere from 321  billion for ten thousand dollars per  borrower all the way up to about 1.6  trillion dollars if all student debt is  cancelled eliminating all federal  student debt could raise inflation by  0.37 to 0.5 percentage points  that's according to the committee for a  responsible federal budget an  independent nonpartisan nonprofit  but some economists say a smaller scale  forgiveness program could have less of  an impact on inflation rates  biden is also considering limiting the  program to borrowers who make less than  125 000 a year according to white house  officials they're considering putting an  income threshold in place so that only  lower income borrowers will benefit as  opposed to higher income borrowers who  may still have student loans but are in  a much better position to pay them off  if you canceled ten thousand dollars  worth of debt and especially if you  included an income threshold the effects  on inflation would be pretty negligible  according to most economists  still some economists say there are  long-term inflationary risks  in the long term  student debt forgiveness could  exacerbate inflationary pressures by  having borrowers treat loans essentially  as grants because they expect those  loans to ultimately be forgiven by the  federal government it could also push up  the price of higher education because  colleges could pretty truthfully say to  their prospective students it doesn't  really matter what our tuition costs  because the federal government is just  going to forgive that debt in the future  anyway  in a statement a spokesperson for the  u.s department of education said our  overall goal is long-lasting change that  reduces indebtedness and makes college  more affordable including through  rulemaking  legal challenges may also complicate the  final decision  according to a legal analysis prepared  by obama's former top education  department lawyer biden could be on  shaky legal ground if he were to pursue  broad-based student debt cancellation by  executive action  lots of people including democrats think  that they don't have the authority to  cancel debt on a mass scale outside of  an existing program like the income  based repayment program  and if he just does it by executive  action president biden runs the risk  that this could get overturned in court  especially by a supreme court that tilts  conservative  biden has long been skeptical of using  his executive authority to wipe away  student loan debt i don't think i have  the authority to do it by signing  still those who are financially tied to  the student loan industry may be in a  position to sue the administration  like loan servicing companies and  investors that own securities backed by  student loans so for instance the  companies that service these loans could  potentially sue because they would be  servicing fewer loans because some of  those loans would be forgiven entirely  people who hold investments that are  based on the debt held by these  borrowers could also sue because their  underlying investments would be affected  if biden chooses to go the regulatory  route there could be other bureaucratic  challenges  the issue for biden if he goes the  regulatory route is that they haven't  really started a rulemaking process for  mass debt cancellation which means that  any future debt cancellation on a mass  scale that went through that process  would be very far down the road because  of how long the rulemaking process takes  implementing a large new government  program may come with logistical hurdles  too for instance a wider forgiveness  policy could require millions of  americans to file paperwork detailing  their income potentially creating a  massive backlog  cancelling debt on this wide of a scale  would be a major logistical lift for the  federal government they would need to  verify people's incomes if they're  putting an income threshold in place  they need to make sure that they're  cancelling the right loans and not  canceling others and all of that would  take a significant amount of time and  it's unclear how fast they would be able  to implement it and whether that would  even happen  in biden's first term  about 40 million borrowers hold about  1.6 trillion dollars in federal student  debt making student debt forgiveness a  potent issue ahead of november's midterm  elections and a pause on federal student  loan payments is set to expire at the  end of august just as campaign season  gets underway if democrats lose one seat  in the senate they will have lost the  majority and if they lose more than a  handful of seats in the house of  representatives they will have lost the  majority there as well which could badly  damage biden's chances of enacting  anything serious in the remaining two  years of his first term  some democrats are urging biden to go  big hoping that a sweeping debt  forgiveness plan can motivate voters who  will decide which party controls  congress  other democrats worry such a move would  alienate voters who don't currently have  student debt or never did  at the same time republicans broadly  oppose any type of mass forgiveness  policy  40 million people is a major portion of  the american electorate making their  student debt payments when they resume  is going to take a significant chunk of  their income every month so it's  something that voters are keenly aware  of if they hold student debt and if they  previously paid off their loans or never  had loans to begin with they might look  at debt cancellation policies and say  well that wasn't around when i was  paying for college  as millions of borrowers await a final  decision the biden administration is  weighing the long-term impacts of a wide  scale student loan forgiveness program  while progressives in congress would  love for congress to do something on  student 